y us  every Sunday night so just a quick  overview overview of the publishing  workflow here's what we ended up with  and this was the quickest way to get  this out so we start with our discover  weekly recommendations which are you  know based on Hadoop jobs so they live  in HDFS and we have a publisher that  uses rabbitmq to paralyze the publishing  so there's two major things that's doing  one is that each of these workers is  going to for each playlist check with  Facebook to see if the user is connected  but also if their image has changed and  if so we'll have to kick off a new image  processing and and transcoding so  transcoding is where you basically  resize it for different platforms and  stuff so we have a separate service for  that and we store those images up on  CDNs around the world and then the main  thing is to interact with the playlist  back end and update the playlists so  ideally we wouldn't have this crazy work  for we'd probably have something like  take the wrecks from HDFS and ingest  them into Cassandra but due to the  complexity of the play us back end we  want with this approach first so that's  pretty much you know that's the high  level it worked out for better for worse  and right off right right out of the  gate we start seeing some great feedback  on Twitter you know pleasantly surprised  a fair system of my taste is scary how  well discover Weekly knows me  it's nice when someone gets you stuff  like that we also had probably the most  press coverage of any release to date  for Spotify around this pretty much all  the major press outlets picked up some  kind of story around discovery weekly  and some are still coming out the other  end of it was  in September we actually had a oops and  didn't update on time and what actually  turned out to be interesting is that  users reacted to this and we could  really tell wow people are really  engaged with this playlist so we saw  stuff like it's 1041 a.m. on Monday and  I haven't seen an update yet I'm having  an existential crisis and stuff like  this threw me into a blind rage when my  playlists didn't show up so yeah so you  know we learned from this some other  things like you know we have to worry  about Sunday night updates and make sure  that our team here in New York is  capable of handling on-call stuff like  that so that's been good and now I'll  hand it back to Chris to talk about  what's next around iterating on content  quality and other things we're gonna do  with discover weekly yeah so so the last  thing is once once we've got this thing  out there it's you know what's the last  step in the last step is really to  continue to iterate so you know we've  released this this great feature but  that's not the end of the game right so  now we get to the really interesting  part where we can continue to iterate on  the quality and improve it and now we're  getting tons of feedback data so we know  what users are listening to we know what  they're skipping we know what they're  saving and we're gonna use all of that  to continue to improve upon discovery  weekly and make it even more awesome so  these ones again okay cool so basically  this is sort of the flow that we're  looking at now so one thing that's super  important with recommendation models is  getting this feedback loop in so once  you've got your recommendation model out  there it's great because now you're  gonna start getting feedback on what  users are using what they're interacting  with saves skips that sort of thing so  this is sort of what the flow looks like  we we train a bunch of collaborative  filtering models we get a bunch of  signals both track signals and user  signals how popular is a song how recent  is it you know maybe you're going to  discover that certain users only want to  see new stuff in their discovered weekly  while other users you know they want to  dip into you know old like Grateful Dead  b-sides or something right so the idea  is to take all the track signals all the  user signals and we're going to combine  them and how are we going to combine  them well we're going to use feedback  data from discover weekly in order to  combine and train those models train  hyper parameters figure out how to  weight different signals and so you end  up with this great feedback  Loup of where you're you know combining  signals you're getting some  recommendations you're putting them out  there you get new feedback you retrain  your models and so you end up in this  continual feedback loop of always kind  of improving your models and putting out  new recommendations one thing that's  super important with this is that all  this feedback data is going to come with  a presentation bias so we learned this  really quickly you know we put discover  weekly out there we start getting  feedback data and now you look at your  recommendation models and you try to  predict you know is this going to be a  skipped song or just going to be a saved  song and what you're going to notice is  that it can't distinguish between the  two so if you tried to build let's say a  under the ROC curve for this it looks  looks like you're doing terrible well  that makes sense because your  recommendation models think that all of  these songs are good on your first  iteration so one thing that becomes  super important is sampling random  negatives and this helps you sort of  step out of that presentation bias a  little bit  so to illustrate this a little bit  better this is kind of a situation I  like to think of it as this sort of  reinforcement learning view so we've got  this sort of current view of the world  right and we're going to get some  positive and negative feedback back from  that and we're gonna use that to sort of  step in the right direction and then  we're gonna get some more feedback and  we're gonna use that to step in another  direction and so it's this sort of  constant flow of stepping in another  direction of the world and we're very  slowly as we gather feedback and I try  to reach the optimal view so this is  kind of a way to think about it I think  so brings me to our last lesson are  actually there's there's one there's one  extra so lesson four is essentially this  in the end you know users know best a B  test everything it doesn't matter how  our offline metrics are performing it  doesn't matter what employees are saying  it doesn't matter what some small group  of 100 users that you're testing what  they're saying at the end of the day the  only thing that matters is your users so  at the end always a/b test everything  and that's what you're going to fit to  and then the final lesson is essentially  this and I think this is really the  story of Discover weekly discovered  weekly wasn't something that was handed  down by executives in the company it  wasn't you know we had this great grand  strategy about building these play  it was going to be amazing and we  included you know hundreds of engineers  and this it's amazing process know he  discovered weekly was you know three  engineers who were noticing some  insights and their data and the features  they were working on and then decided to  build something so I think the real  magic of discover weekly to me was that  it was a bottom it was bottom-up  innovation so I think this is super  important I think if you empower  bottom-up innovation in your  organization amazing things will happen  so this isn't to say that you shouldn't  have long term planning and you  shouldn't you shouldn't do that but I  think empowering bottom-up innovation  and trusting your engineers is super  important so that's it thank you very  much I just want to mention also we're  hiring machine learning and data  engineers so find us come chat with us  Thanks  we've got a few minutes to answer a few  questions yeah so I'll run the mic  around if you're easier in hi was a  great talk thanks my question is how  often do you have to relearn your  machine learning models 